     1	# records_txn — fail-closed record updates for Planner records scripts
     2	
     3	`records_txn.py` (standard library only, Python ≥ 3.9, git ≥ 2.15 for `require_git`) is the shared helper asked
     4	for by Codex reviews *Planner records scripts P1* and *Planner records tooling P2*. A records script **declares**
     5	everything, then calls `commit()` once; any exception before `commit()` means nothing was written. Every refusal is
     6	an explicit `Refused` (a `SystemExit` subclass: exit 1, clean message, not swallowed by `except Exception`); there
     7	is no `assert`, so `python -O` changes nothing.
     8	
     9	## API
    10	
    11	```python
    12	import sys; sys.path.insert(0, "<the stable, reviewed location of records_txn.py>")
    13	import records_txn as rt
    14	
    15	txn = rt.Transaction(state_dir=os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/flac_records_txn"))  # refuses while unfinished
    16	txn.expect_date("2026-11-05")                # MANDATORY: today in New York now, and again under the commit lock
    17	txn.iso, txn.hm                              # one clock read: '2026-11-05T18:30:00-05:00', '18:30 EST' (zone derived)
    18	rev = txn.require_text(REV, "<full sha256>", must_start="**Reviewer:** OpenAI Codex",
    19	                       must_contain=["**Round:** C3c", "**Verdict:** REQUEST-CHANGES"])   # verified str
    20	raw = txn.require_source(LOG, "<full sha256>")             # verified bytes; derive every recorded fact from these
    21	git = txn.require_git(REPO, branch="localization-exp", head="<40-hex>", upstream="origin/localization-exp",
    22	                      clean_paths=[NOTE, HAND])            # read-only git; returns {head, branch, ...}; archived
    23	hand = txn.text(HAND)                        # destination text, read ONCE; optional 2nd arg pins its sha256
    24	txn.replace_once(HAND, old, new)             # refuses unless `old` occurs exactly once (overlaps counted)
    25	txn.append(NOTE, "\n## ...\n")               # refuses unless the file and the text both end with "\n"
    26	txn.create(NEW, data, parents=False)         # bytes or str; refuses if NEW exists (even as a dangling symlink)
    27	txn.copy_verified(SRC, DST, "<full sha256>", parents=True) # DST gets exactly the verified bytes
    28	done = txn.commit()                          # returns the archived manifest (the audit record)
    29	```
    30	
    31	`require_git` runs only `rev-parse`, `symbolic-ref` and `status` (with `--no-optional-locks`, so the index is never
    32	rewritten, and `core.fsmonitor=false`) and at least one check is required. Each clean path must exist and be
    33	tracked, unmodified and not ignored; pathspecs are literal. The same checks run again under the lock, and any change
    34	since declaration (a new commit, a switched branch, a dirty path) refuses. Several edits to one file chain in
    35	memory; two spellings of one path are one destination; a file read with `text()` and left unchanged is still
    36	compare-and-swap checked, but never rewritten. A transaction is single-use.
    37	
    38	## What `commit()` does
    39	
    40	1. Refuses without `expect_date()`. Takes `flock(LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB)` on `<state_dir>/records_txn.lock` (a second
    41	   writer is refused, not queued); refuses while a recovery manifest exists. Re-checks every destination: same
    42	   sha256, one hard link, not a symlink, new files still absent. Then the date (fresh clock) and every
    43	   `require_git` check. **Nothing is written before this point.**
    44	2. Writes the recovery manifest `<state_dir>/records_txn.recovery.json` (`O_EXCL`) **before anything else**: every
    45	   planned install with its temp path and old / new sha256, the directories it will create, the verified sources,
    46	   the git state, date, argv, host, pid. Fsyncs it and the state dir. A kill from here on leaves only marked debris.
    47	3. Creates the planned directories; stages each output to `.<name>.<txid>.records_txn.tmp` in its destination's own
    48	   directory (`O_EXCL`, old mode kept, fsync, read back); fsyncs the directories.
    49	4. Last look: every destination unchanged, links included. Then installs, new files first. Each edit is
    50	   re-checked (bytes and `st_nlink == 1`) immediately before its rename. **The install-attempted phase begins
    51	   immediately before the first `link(2)` / `rename(2)` is invoked**, never after it returns.
    52	5. Fsyncs the directories; archives: `link` the manifest to `<state_dir>/done/<txid>.json`, fsync `done/`, only
    53	   then unlink the active mark and fsync the state dir. If that last fsync fails, the mark is restored.
    54	
    55	A failure before step 2 writes nothing. A failure in steps 2–4 before the first install attempt removes the temps
    56	and directories of this transaction, fsyncs their parents and only then removes the mark. If any of that cannot be
    57	removed, the mark stays (`RecoveryNeeded`). From the first install attempt on, every failure keeps the mark and
    58	raises `RecoveryNeeded`. That includes an operation that happened and then raised, an asynchronous exception, a
    59	syscall that failed without effect (conservative by design), and any archive step.
    60	
    61	## Recovery (never automatic)
    62	
    63	`python records_txn.py status <state_dir>` (exit 1 while unfinished) lists each planned install as
    64	`[installed]`, `[pending]` (destination untouched, temp complete), `[unstaged]` (destination untouched, temp
    65	missing or partial) or `[CONFLICT]`, plus the planned directories. It says "nothing was installed" when that holds.
    66	Finish a pending replacement with `mv TEMP DST`, a new file with `ln TEMP DST && rm TEMP`, or discard with
    67	`rm TEMP`. Restore installed entries from git if the whole set must go. Then move the manifest to
    68	`<state_dir>/done/`. Re-run the script: its anchors re-validate against whatever is now on disk.
    69	
    70	## Every records script must
    71	
    72	- Use one transaction for every destination it touches, and the shared `state_dir`
    73	  `~/.local/state/flac_records_txn`, which is created once with `mkdir -p`. Write no destination directly.
    74	- Call `expect_date()`; use `txn.iso` / `txn.hm`; never type a zone label.
    75	- Pin every source by its **full** sha256 and require its round / verdict / content. Derive claims from the returned
    76	  bytes or text; historic event times come from those sources, not from typed literals.
    77	- Declare branch / HEAD / upstream / clean-path expectations with `require_git`. Run `git pull --rebase`, the
    78	  commit (with the trailer) and the push outside the helper, teeing the exact commands and results to a log.
    79	- Stop on `RecoveryNeeded`; inspect `status`; resolve by hand before any later records script.
    80	
    81	## Limits
    82	
    83	- The lock binds only `records_txn` users of the same `state_dir`. An editor or git writing a destination is
    84	  caught by the compare-and-swap checks up to the final window between an edit's last check and its `rename(2)`.
    85	  Linux has no compare-and-swap rename.
    86	- The install is not atomic across files. Durability rests on fsync ordering: the tests pin the call order, not
    87	  behaviour under power loss.
    88	- Destinations must be UTF-8 regular files with a single link, on filesystems with hard links; sources are read
    89	  whole into memory. Mode is kept; owner, ACLs and xattrs are not.
    90	
    91	Tests: `CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES= <flac python> -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider <this dir>`. They use tmp_path only;
    92	the `require_git` tests build a throwaway repository there with `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null`.

